      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 18 and 42, and then find the product of these two values.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 18 and 42:\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine the GCD by taking the lowest power of all prime factors that appear in both numbers. The only common prime factors of 18 and 42 are 2 and 3. The lowest power of 2 in both factorizations is 1, and the lowest power of 3 is 1. Therefore, the GCD is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the LCM by taking the highest power of all prime factors that appear in the factorizations. The prime factorisons from both numbers are 2, 3, and 7. The highest power of 2 is \\(2^1\\), the highest power of 3 is \\(3^2\\), and the highest power of 7 is \\(7^1\\). Therefore, the LCM is:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\boxed{756}.",
