      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we first need to determine the GCD and LCM of these two numbers.\n\n### Step 1: Finding the GCD of 18 and 42\n\nThe prime factorizations of 18 and 42 are:\n\\[18 = 2 \\times 3^2\\]\n\\[42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\\]\n\nThe GCD is found by taking the lowest power of all prime factors common to both numbers. The common prime factors are 2 and 3:\n\\[18 = 2^1 \\times 3^2\\]\n\\[42 = 2^1 \\times 3^1 \\times 7^0\\]\nSo, the GCD is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Finding the LCM of 18 and 42\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of all prime factors present in either number. The prime factorizations are:\n\\[18 = 2^1 \\times 3^2\\]\n\\[42 = 2^1 \\times 3^1 \\times 7^1\\]\nSo, the LCM is:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculating the product of the GCD and the LCM\n\nNow, we multiply the GCD by the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n\\]\n\nInstead of calculating manually, we can use Python to verify and calculate the final product.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given numbers\na = 18\nb = 42\n\n# Calculate GCD\ngcd = math.gcd(a, b)\n\n# Calculate LCM using LCM formula: LCM(a, b) = (a * b) // GCD(a, b)\nlcm = (a * b) // gcd\n\n# Calculate the product of GCD and LCM\nproduct = gcd * lcm\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n756\n```\nThe product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
